# Line endings are not a preference here; they are a correctness issue.
#
# ~600 tests read source files as strings and assert on exact markup
# ("{mode === \"signup\" && (\n          <PasswordField"). A working copy that
# Git checked out with CRLF fails those tests locally while CI (Linux, LF)
# passes — so a developer sees red that is not real, learns to ignore red, and
# then misses the one that is. On 2026-09-28, 919 of 1,192 tracked text files
# on the owner's PC were CRLF in the working tree and LF in the index, because
# core.autocrlf=true rewrites every file on checkout.
#
# `eol=lf` here beats core.autocrlf on every machine: Git checks out LF and
# commits LF, so the bytes a test reads are the bytes CI reads. Windows
# editors, Node, and Next all handle LF. tests/line-endings.test.ts fails if a
# CRLF file ever reaches the index anyway.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries — never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.jar binary
*.gz binary
*.p12 binary
*.mobileprovision binary
*.pkpass binary
